## Why This Is Asked

This question tests advanced knowledge of schema evolution, cross-service coordination, and zero-downtime migration strategies in distributed systems. It evaluates understanding of how to evolve data contracts in a CNPA stack (HTTP API, PostgreSQL, Kafka, Redis) while maintaining system reliability and data consistency.

## Key Concepts

- Avro schema evolution and Schema Registry for managing schema versions
- Backward and forward compatibility in distributed data pipelines
- Zero-downtime migrations with phased rollout strategies
- Data backfill techniques and drift detection
- Producer/consumer tolerance for handling both old and new field representations
- Circuit breaking and canary deployments for safe migration

## Code Example

```javascript
// Avro schema excerpt (new field with default)
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "UserEvent",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "userId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "event", "type": "string"},
    {
      "name": "user_tier",
      "type": ["null", "string"],
      "default": null,
      "doc": "User tier level (free, premium, enterprise)"
    }
  ],
  "doc": "User event schema with optional user_tier field"
}

// Producer: Emit events with optional field
const produceUserEvent = async (userId, event, userTier = null) => {
  const event = {
    userId,
    event,
    user_tier: userTier  // Optional; defaults to null for old consumers
  };
  await kafkaProducer.send({
    topic: 'user-events',
    messages: [{ value: avroSerializer.serialize(event) }]
  });
};

// Consumer: Handle both old and new schemas gracefully
const consumeUserEvent = async (message) => {
  const event = avroDeserializer.deserialize(message.value);
  // Safely access optional field with fallback
  const tier = event.user_tier ?? 'free';
  console.log(`User ${event.userId} at tier: ${tier}`);
};
```

